<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Klemens von Metternich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1773">1773</a>–<a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Prince Metternich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Klemens von Metternich was Austrian foreign minister from 1809 and chancellor from 1821 until 1848. A leading figure at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1814–1815, he shaped a conservative European order based on the balance of power and cooperation among monarchies. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the German Confederation he suppressed liberal and national movements through censorship and police, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a> forced him to flee.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich.jpg" alt="Klemens von Metternich" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Lawrence · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Metternich_by_Lawrence.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Klemens Metternich was born on 15 May 1773 into the old Rhenish House of Metternich, the son of Franz Georg Karl, a diplomat who had passed from the service of the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> to that of the imperial court. He was named after Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clemens-wenceslaus-of-saxony">Clemens Wenceslaus of Saxony</a>, the archbishop-elector of Trier and his father's former employer.</p><p>He spoke French better than German and later studied at the universities of Strasbourg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. In October 1794 a French army annexed all the family estates except Königswart in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. On 27 September 1795 he married Countess Eleonore von Kaunitz-Rietberg, a match arranged by his mother that introduced him to Viennese society.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Metternich served as Austrian minister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> from 1801 and then as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s France from August 1806. After the Austrian defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wagram">Battle of Wagram</a> in 1809, Johann Philipp Stadion resigned as foreign minister, and Metternich became foreign minister on 8 October. He arranged a détente with France that included the marriage of Napoleon to the archduchess Marie Louise, then brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-sixth-coalition">War of the Sixth Coalition</a> on the Allied side.</p><p>He was made a prince in October 1813. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, which gathered from autumn 1814, he had his aide Friedrich von Gentz named secretary to the negotiations.</p><p>After 1815 he sought to preserve the existing social hierarchy and the authority of legitimate sovereigns. Wanting a strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> to counterbalance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, he opposed Greek nationalism. At home he used censorship and a wide-ranging spy network to suppress unrest, and he crushed nationalist revolts in Austrian northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In late February 1848 Joseph Radetsky placed Lombardy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> under martial law as disturbances spread, and the revolution forced Metternich to resign. He travelled through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, arriving on 20 April, and lived in exile until 1851. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>'s press accused him of embezzlement and bribery, but an investigation cleared him of the more extreme charges.</p><p>He returned to the Viennese court to advise Emperor Franz Josef and died on 11 June 1859, aged 86. Historians agree on his skill as a diplomat and his dedication to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a>. Supporters note that his system helped prevent major wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, while critics call him a stumbling block to reform in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Klemens von Metternich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Public life under <b>Metternich</b> was constrained by censorship, so the comfortable private culture of the Biedermeier middle classes flourished instead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>German patriots had hoped for a national state, but Austria under Prince <b>Klemens von Metternich</b>, the middle states such as Bavaria and Württemberg, and the great powers all preferred a league that would preserve the sovereignty of its members and the balance of Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When news of the revolution in France reached Vienna, the Austrian chancellor <b>Klemens von Metternich</b> is said to have exclaimed, in French, &quot;well, my dear, it's all over!&quot;; a few weeks later he resigned and went into exile.</blockquote><a href="/movement/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848 · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince <b>Klemens von Metternich</b> built the monarchist Conservative Order of the Congress of Vienna to balance power in Europe and suppress republican and nationalist movements.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom was mainly an administrative label: it was divided into two governorates, at Laibach and Trieste, each run by Austrian officials under the system of <b>Metternich</b>, with provincial estates of little power.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-illyria">Kingdom of Illyria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.36,7.6&amp;year=1828" aria-label="Koblenz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Klemens von Metternich"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5413 5797h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5413 5797h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.36,7.6&amp;year=1828">What was here in 1828? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
